WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.950
A "Kraven the Hunter" movie is finally on
the prowl.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:08.490
Sony has debuted the first trailer for the
newest entry in its line of Spider-Man related

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:14.143
films, so it's time to track down all the
movie connections and comic nods on this flick's trail.

00:00:14.143 --> 00:00:19.380
In the comics, Kraven powers up with a special
herbal cocktail that boosts his physical attributes

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.660
far beyond normal.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.779
While the upper limits of his strength are
by no means at Spider-Man's level, he can

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:28.939
definitely hold his own against many other
superpowered folks strength-wise.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:34.710
Live-action adaptations of comic book characters
might not always share the exact power set

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.930
of their print counterparts, but from the
looks of it, Taylor-Johnson's Kraven will

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.890
be roughly as fast, agile, and powerful as
the comics one.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:47.250
In the trailer, we see him chase a car that's
attempting to speed away in reverse, then

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.000
jump on top of it and tear his way in.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.500
The superhuman feats on display seem to be
roughly in the same ballpark as the character's

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.390
traditional powers, and since the trailer
also makes clear that Kraven has greatly enhanced

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:03.730
senses, we just might be looking at a very
faithful adaptation ... at least, in this

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.460
particular respect.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.600
While the trailer suggests that Kraven's powers
are similar to the comic book version, the

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.049
way he acquires them seems to be pretty far
removed from what fans are used to.

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:18.060
Instead of the herbal serum that gives him
powers in the comics, the implication here

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.970
seems to be that Kraven gets his power boost
from lion blood.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:26.630
As young Kraven hunts a lion with his father,
he hesitates to fire his weapon, and the animal

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:27.689
mauls the boy.

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:31.890
As the injured Kraven lies on the ground,
a drop of the lion's blood falls in one of

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:37.040
his open wounds, jump-starting something that
seems to revive him and give him superpowers.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.240
An accidental blood transfusion with a large
cat seems like an odd origin story, to say

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.240
the least.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.189
It seems that the trailer is deliberately
missing a few steps in the process, possibly

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:49.490
to avoid revealing major plot points.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:53.189
Or hey, maybe the lion just happens to be
radioactive.

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:57.369
It's clear from the initial trailer that "Kraven
the Hunter" is leaning hard into the family

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:02.189
drama element of its titular character's backstory,
and it seems his father isn't the only blood

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:04.240
relative he'll be at odds with.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.030
Indeed, audiences get their first peek at
Kraven's half-brother Dmitri, a character

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.620
far better known for his alter ego as Spider-Man
supervillain The Chameleon.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.500
In the trailer, fans learn that the character
doesn't approve of his half-brother's brutal

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:23.265
quest for justice, indicating that he may
have stronger moral fiber than his comic book counterpart.

00:02:23.265 --> 00:02:27.230
While the trailer doesn't include a look at
the Chameleon's trademark skills as a master

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.990
of disguise, reports from Deadline and other
outlets indicates that the character delves

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.540
headfirst into the persona at some point in
the movie.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.220
Of course, "Kraven the Hunter" is already
dealing with a laundry list of antagonists,

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.920
so it remains to be seen just how extensive
of a role the face-changing villain plays

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:45.920
in this film.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.800
In any case, viewers would do well to second-guess
whether certain characters are really themselves

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.080
on-screen, or if it's Chameleon impersonating
them.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.990
If there's one Spider-Man villain with an
exceptionally spotty track record in cinema,

00:02:58.990 --> 00:02:59.990
it's the Rhino.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:04.840
This behemoth of a foe has appeared a few
times in various movies, with a version portrayed

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:10.110
by Paul Giamatti briefly popping up in "The
Amazing Spider-Man 2" and a couple of multiversal

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:14.090
variations getting cameos in "Spider-Man:
Across the Spider-Verse."

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:18.690
But audiences haven't really gotten a chance
to see the character rise as a legitimate

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:19.690
villainous threat.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.980
However, the first trailer for "Kraven the
Hunter" indicates that the movie may be giving

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:29.060
him a bigger spotlight this time around — along
with, on some level, returning him to his

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.360
comic book roots.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:35.800
The final moments of the trailer introduce
audiences to Alessandro Nivola's take on the Rhino.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:40.280
Though viewers don't get to see him go full
beast-mode, they do witness him activate his

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:45.129
powers by way of a self-applied injection,
giving him his signature tough gray armor.

00:03:45.129 --> 00:03:49.689
This sequence shows a Rhino a bit closer to
the original comics conception rather than

00:03:49.689 --> 00:03:53.990
the giant robot suit that Giamatti wore in
"The Amazing Spider-Man 2."

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:58.770
While the trailer's Rhino tease may have fans
chomping at the bit for more, it's wise to

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:04.394
temper expectations on how many superpowered
shenanigans the character will actually get up to.

00:04:04.394 --> 00:04:08.590
In an interview with The Hollywood Reporter,
Nivola said that he only transforms at the

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:09.870
very end of the film.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:14.530
Based on his quote, it seems that this scene
may actually come from the film's conclusion,

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:19.239
thereby setting a full-fledged Rhino up for
a "Kraven the Hunter" sequel.

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:23.880
Sony's Marvel movies have generally delved
into darker and more graphic subject matter

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.820
than their Marvel Cinematic Universe equivalents,
but the films have nonetheless remained firmly

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.080
rooted in PG-13 territory.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.470
That's what made it rather surprising when
Kraven actor Aaron Taylor-Johnson revealed

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:41.130
at CinemaCon 2023 that "Kraven the Hunter"
would land a strong R-rating.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.949
Fans may have had their doubts, but the trailer
definitively puts any skepticism on that front

00:04:45.949 --> 00:04:46.949
to rest.

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:52.160
Throughout the trailer, audiences see Kraven
take out a number of hired henchmen in spectacularly

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:53.350
gory fashion.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:58.220
Throats are sliced, necks are stabbed, and
chests are shot through with crossbows.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:02.660
The lead even bites off part of a guy's face
at one point, just to really hammer the point

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:07.030
home that, yes, "Kraven the Hunter" isn't
pulling any punches with its bloody brand

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:08.030
of violence.

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:13.550
Oh, and the trailer shows a young Kraven getting
mauled by a lion, so toss graphic child peril

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:18.340
on the list of "definitely-not-PG-13" stuff
in here, too.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.550
Most movies adapted from comic books alter
things here and there to adjust the property

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.410
from the page to the screen.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:28.800
Sometimes, in the interest of telling a good
story, writers and filmmakers will alter the

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.830
origins or setting of a character.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:35.449
In this case, the writers went a bit further,
and appear to have fundamentally altered the

00:05:35.449 --> 00:05:37.320
core motivations of the character.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.750
In the trailer, we see that Kraven is hunting
for one reason.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:45.097
"My father puts evil into the world.

00:05:45.097 --> 00:05:47.360
I take it out."

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.199
Since this is an origin story, we can assume
this mostly occurs before he turns into the

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:56.470
brutish hunter hellbent on catching his most
pursued prey, Spider-Man.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:06:01.910
However, in the comics, Kraven is a depraved
man beaten in childhood by his alcoholic father

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.530
and who, in turn, beats his half-brother.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:09.350
He turns to big game hunting and downs every
coveted animal on his list before turning

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:11.130
his attention to Spider-Man.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:14.819
Since the superhero represents the animal
that haunted his dreams since his mother's

00:06:14.819 --> 00:06:17.970
time in an asylum, it becomes a personal mission.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:22.569
While there are shreds of that origin represented
in the trailer, like his father as a villain,

00:06:22.569 --> 00:06:27.380
being tormented by spiders, and so on, the
film seems to be taking the anti-hero approach

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.360
to the famous Spider-Man antagonist.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.210
Is Kraven destined for a showdown with Spider-Man?

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:37.750
That remains to be seen, but the trailer for
his upcoming solo movie does tease the hunter's

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:40.680
obsession with the friendly neighborhood superhero.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.720
Toward the end of the teaser, Kraven is shown
lying on the ground as spiders rush toward

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:45.900
him from the trees.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.150
The sequence might help to remind viewers
that this is a "Spider-Man" Universe movie,

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:52.889
but it might also reveal more about Kraven's
psyche.

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:57.389
The scene is clearly done in homage to Marvel's
"Kraven's Last Hunt" storyline.

00:06:57.389 --> 00:07:02.020
This tale chronicles the titular villain's
attempts to dispose of Spidey and take his

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:05.890
place, and it's widely regarded as one of
the best stories in "Spider-Man" history.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:10.860
Furthermore, the scene in the trailer is a
reference to "Web of Spider-Man #31," which

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:16.210
features a panel that depicts Kraven munching
on arachnids as they cover his entire body.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:20.030
Is the "Kraven the Hunter" trailer trying
to tell us that this version of the character

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:23.500
will also be hell-bent on eating Spider-Man
down the line?

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:28.030
With a piece of Venom's symbiote now in the
Marvel Cinematic Universe alongside Peter

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.150
Parker, anything is possible.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:34.990
While it hasn't officially been confirmed
yet, seemingly everyone knows that Ariana

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.479
de Bose is set to play Calypso in "Kraven
the Hunter."

00:07:38.479 --> 00:07:42.710
However, if there are still any lingering
doubts about that fact, the teaser should

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:43.710
clear them up.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:47.740
In the comics, Calypso is a voodoo priestess
and Kraven's love interest.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:53.139
She takes great delight in nurturing his psychopathic
tendencies and fueling his hatred of Spider-Man

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:57.040
to a troubling extent, so at least they've
got hobbies in common.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:02.599
However, if Sony insists on turning Kraven
into a sympathetic antihero for his big on-screen

00:08:02.599 --> 00:08:06.789
outing, it makes sense to give Calypso a more
lighthearted makeover as well.

00:08:06.789 --> 00:08:11.449
The trailer implies that de Bose's character
will be on the protagonist's side, which only

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:15.740
lends credence to the rumors that she's playing
some version of the voodoo priestess.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:20.900
Furthermore, Calypso often sports a gold necklace
around her neck — similar to the one de

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.770
Bose wears in the "Kraven the Hunter" trailer.

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:28.419
While it was impressive when the studio booked
Aaron Taylor-Johnson for the "Kraven the Hunter"

00:08:28.419 --> 00:08:33.370
lead, Sony also locked in another compelling
name from the indie film realm for a potentially

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:35.990
major supporting role in Christopher Abbott.

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:41.314
Abbott's role has yet to be confirmed, but
rumors abound he's playing Spidey foe The Foreigner.

00:08:41.314 --> 00:08:47.250
Perhaps best known for roles in HBO's "Girls,"
and the 2019 mini-series "Catch-22," Abbott

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:52.110
has also wowed in projects like "It Comes
At Night," "First Man," and "Possessor."

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.240
"Kraven" is undoubtedly his biggest film to
date, and if Abbott is playing the Foreigner

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:59.760
in the blockbuster, he'll no doubt bring something
unique to the mix.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.620
Fans would probably not call the Foreigner
a major Spider-Man baddie; he's probably best

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.180
known for once being married to fellow mercenary
Silver Sable.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:11.320
But minor villains have gotten big movie roles
in the past.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:15.540
C-lister The Spot was the key villain in "Across
the Spider-Verse," after all.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:20.380
It remains to be seen how big a role the Foreigner
might play in "Kraven the Hunter."

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:24.660
After giving a look at Kraven's origin, most
of the trailer is dedicated to showing how

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:28.220
many different people the grown-up version
of Sergei can kill.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:33.060
But one element of the character sticking
out like a sore thumb is Sergei's manner of

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.640
speech, because he appears to have an American
accent.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.120
That shouldn't be the case, given that his
entire family is Russian, a detail that's

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.790
been preserved from the comics, as we hear
his father speak in a Russian accent multiple

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:48.180
times during the first half of the trailer.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.520
"Power is about strength.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:56.960
If you show weakness, you will give our enemies
an opening."

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:00.959
Perhaps Kraven was shipped off to a boarding
school, where one could assume he picked up

00:10:00.959 --> 00:10:02.240
his American accent.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:06.430
If that's the case, it's unclear whether he
was shipped off to the boarding school before

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:11.330
or after the accident that gives him his powers,
but by the time he's a teenager, Sergei's

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:14.470
speech is clearly not with a Russian accent.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.370
"She died because you sent her away."

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:22.740
"Kraven the Hunter" is the next chapter of
Sony's Spider-Man-less Spidey-Verse, so it

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.459
makes sense for there to be some connections
to "Venom" and "Morbius."

00:10:26.459 --> 00:10:31.510
The trailer does seem to connect to one unseen
character first mentioned in the first "Venom."

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:34.320
A man enters an office to see Mr. Taglin.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:39.110
Unfortunately, Kraven's already killed Mr.
Taglin and shoots the man with a crossbow.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:42.470
It certainly seems like Mr. Taglin was important…

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:45.100
"Mr. Taglin?

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:46.209
Mr. Taglin.

00:10:47.906 --> 00:10:49.170
Where's Mr. Taglin?"

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.380
Another Taglin was referenced in "Venom."

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:57.140
In that movie, Lee Taglin is an employee at
Michelinie &amp; McFarlane who works with Anne

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:59.950
Weying, Eddie Brock's ex-fiancée.

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:04.591
This is the law firm that represents the foundation
responsible for capturing the symbiotes, one

00:11:04.591 --> 00:11:05.980
of which was Venom.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:11.540
It's possible that the Mr. Taglin whom Kraven
killed is the same one who worked with Anne.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:14.029
After all, why focus on the name so much?

00:11:14.029 --> 00:11:18.960
Or perhaps it's someone who's related to Lee
Taglin, or an entirely different person.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.770
Either way, a connection certainly seems likely,
especially given how often the name is mentioned

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:28.850
in the scene.

